<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding the derivative of the function h(x) using the quotient rule.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the derivative of \( \displaystyle h({@var@}) = {@hx@}\)</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle h'({@var@}) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To find the derivative of the function \( \displaystyle h({@var@}) = {@hx@}\), we will use the quotient rule. </p>
<p>The quotient rule states that if you have a function \( \displaystyle h(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\), then its derivative \(h'(x)\) is given by:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle h'(x) = \frac{f'(x)g(x) - f(x)g'(x)}{(g(x))^2}\)</p>

<p>In our case, \( \displaystyle f({@var@}) = {@fx@}\) and \( \displaystyle g({@var@}) = {@gx@}\). Now, let's find the derivatives of \(f({@var@})\) and \(g({@var@}):\)</p>

<p> \( \displaystyle f'({@var@}) = \frac{d}{dx}({@fx@}) = {@diff_fx@}\)</p>

<p> \( \displaystyle g'({@var@}) = \frac{d}{dx}({@gx@}) = {@diff_gx@}\)</p>

<p>Now, apply the quotient rule:</p>

<p>\(
\displaystyle
\begin{align}
h'({@var@}) &= \frac{({@diff_fx@})({@gx@}) - ({@fx@})({@diff_gx@})}{({@gx@})^2 }\\
           &= \frac{{@expand(diff_fx*gx)@}-{@expand(fx*diff_gx)@}}{({@gx@})^2}  \\
         &={@ta@}
\end{align}
\)</p>

<p>So, the derivative of \( \displaystyle h({@var@}) = {@hx@}\) is \( \displaystyle h'({@var@}) = {@ta@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
